WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
You already know that published statements show the prior year beside the current one, because the standards require comparatives. Horizontal analysis is what an analyst does with that second column once it is there.
3.1  Three ways to state a difference
Take one line and two years. There are three honest ways to say how it moved, and the exam uses all three.
The absolute change is the later amount minus the earlier one, in currency. Orontes's cash rose by 7,302,100 − 2,100,000 = 5,202,100 dollars. This is the figure that matters when you are asking whether the movement is large enough to care about.
The percentage change is the absolute change divided by the earlier amount. For cash that is 5,202,100 ÷ 2,100,000 = 247.7 per cent. This is the figure that lets you compare movements in lines of very different size.
The trend index sets the base year to 100 and expresses every later year against it. Cash at the end of 2025 is 7,302,100 ÷ 2,100,000 = 347.7 on a 2024 base of 100. The index and the percentage change say the same thing: an index of 347.7 is a rise of 247.7 per cent.
EXAM TRAP   The denominator is the earlier year
Percentage change divides by the earlier amount, not the later one and not the average.
An index of 347.7 means a rise of 247.7 per cent. Subtract 100 to get the change.
A percentage change cannot be calculated when the earlier amount is zero, and it is meaningless when the earlier amount is negative. Say "not meaningful" rather than inventing a figure.
Figure F503-02 sets the three out on one line.
Figure F503-02. Three statements of one movement
@table
Measure | How it is calculated | Orontes cash | What it is good for
Absolute change | Later − earlier | 5,202,100 | Is the movement big enough to matter?
Percentage change | (Later − earlier) ÷ earlier | 247.7 per cent | Comparing lines of different size
Trend index | Later ÷ base, × 100 | 347.7 | Following one line over several years
@end
A large percentage on a small line is the commonest way for horizontal analysis to mislead. Orontes's assets held for sale went from nothing to $46,000, which is an infinite percentage rise on a line that is one-tenth of one per cent of the company. Trading securities rose 5.0 per cent, on $40,000. Neither tells you anything. The discipline is to read the absolute change and the percentage change together, and the exam sets questions that punish reading only one.
