3.2  Horizontal analysis of the balance sheet
Figure F503-03 is Orontes's balance sheet with both an absolute and a percentage column. Read it with the common-size column of Chapter 2 beside you: the two answer different questions about the same statement.
Figure F503-03. Orontes Foods, Inc. — horizontal analysis of the balance sheet, 2025 against 2024
@table
Line | 2025 | 2024 | Change | Per cent
Cash | 7,302,100 | 2,100,000 | 5,202,100 | 247.7
Trading securities | 42,000 | 40,000 | 2,000 | 5.0
Accounts receivable, net | 4,144,000 | 3,760,000 | 384,000 | 10.2
Inventory | 2,450,000 | 2,300,000 | 150,000 | 6.5
Prepaid expenses | 204,000 | 180,000 | 24,000 | 13.3
Total current assets | 14,188,100 | 8,380,000 | 5,808,100 | 69.3
Equity-method investments | 1,807,000 | 500,000 | 1,307,000 | 261.4
Property, plant and equipment, net | 14,802,000 | 16,000,000 | (1,198,000) | (7.5)
Goodwill | 300,000 | 950,000 | (650,000) | (68.4)
TOTAL ASSETS | 31,672,600 | 26,445,000 | 5,227,600 | 19.8
Accounts payable | 2,050,000 | 1,900,000 | 150,000 | 7.9
Accrued liabilities | 900,000 | 850,000 | 50,000 | 5.9
Total current liabilities | 4,280,000 | 3,570,000 | 710,000 | 19.9
Long-term debt, less current portion | 6,000,000 | 5,600,000 | 400,000 | 7.1
Total liabilities | 11,279,625 | 10,070,000 | 1,209,625 | 12.0
Common stock, $1 par | 8,100,000 | 8,000,000 | 100,000 | 1.3
Additional paid-in capital | 5,400,000 | 4,000,000 | 1,400,000 | 35.0
Retained earnings | 6,894,100 | 4,375,000 | 2,519,100 | 57.6
Total stockholders' equity | 20,392,975 | 16,375,000 | 4,017,975 | 24.5
@end
The company grew by 19.8 per cent in a year, and the horizontal column says where the growth came from and where it went.
It came from three places. Operations generated cash: retained earnings rose by $2,519,100, which is net income of $2,969,100 less the dividends declared. Owners put money in: common stock and additional paid-in capital together rose by $1,500,000. And the bank lent a little more: long-term debt rose by $400,000.
It went into two places. Cash, by far: $5,202,100 of the $5,227,600 increase in total assets is cash. And an investment: the equity-method holding rose by $1,307,000, which was the purchase of the stake in Jordan Glass Co.
Plant fell by $1,198,000, which is the year's depreciation of $2,100,000 less the $3,000,000 of new equipment, less the carrying amount of what was sold. Goodwill fell by $650,000 because of the impairment.
Nothing in the percentage column is as instructive as the two largest entries read together. Total assets rose 19.8 per cent; total liabilities rose 12.0 per cent; equity rose 24.5 per cent. The company grew and became less reliant on borrowing at the same time, which is what the common-size column showed from the other side when liabilities fell from 38.1 to 35.6 per cent of assets.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: one line, three statements
Step 1. Absolute. Accounts receivable, net: 4,144,000 − 3,760,000 = 384,000. The company is owed $384,000 more than a year ago.
Step 2. Percentage. 384,000 ÷ 3,760,000 = 10.2 per cent. Receivables grew by about a tenth.
Step 3. In context. Total assets grew 19.8 per cent, so receivables grew more slowly than the company. That is why the common-size column showed receivables falling from 14.2 per cent of assets to 13.1 per cent.
Check. Three numbers, one fact: receivables rose in dollars, rose by a tenth, and fell as a share of the company. All three are true, and a reader who has only one of them can be misled by any of the others.
YOUR TURN   Complete the horizontal columns
Figure F503-04 gives four lines. The first is done. Fill in the absolute change and the percentage change, rounding the percentage to one decimal. Answers are at the end of the chapter.
Figure F503-04. Your turn: the horizontal columns (complete the blanks)
@table
Line | 2025 | 2024 | Change | Per cent
Inventory | 2,450,000 | 2,300,000 | 150,000 | 6.5
Accounts payable | 2,050,000 | 1,900,000 | ________ | ________
Long-term debt, less current portion | 6,000,000 | 5,600,000 | ________ | ________
Goodwill | 300,000 | 950,000 | ________ | ________
Total stockholders' equity | 20,392,975 | 16,375,000 | ________ | ________
@end
