3.4  Choosing the base year
The index depends entirely on which year is set to 100, and the choice is not neutral.
A base year should be normal. If the base year was a year of strike, flood, acquisition or impairment, every later index is measured against a distorted figure and every later year looks better or worse than it is. Had Orontes used 2025 as its base, the impairment would sit in the denominator and the next three years of operating income would all show handsome growth for no reason connected to trading.
A base year should be far enough back to show a cycle and close enough to be comparable. Three to five years is the usual range. Ten years back the business may be a different business; one year back there is no trend to see.
A base year must be restated when the company restates. If a prior year is restated for a change in accounting principle or for an error, the index must use the restated figure, or the series mixes two measurement bases. Chapter 18 returns to this.
Figure F503-07 shows what the choice does to one series.
Figure F503-07. Orontes operating income on two different bases
@table
Year | Operating income | Index, 2023 = 100 | Index, 2024 = 100
2023 | 3,520,000 | 100.0 | 87.9
2024 | 4,004,000 | 113.8 | 100.0
2025 | 4,176,000 | 118.6 | 104.3
@end
Both columns are correct and neither is a trick. The first says operating income is up 18.6 per cent over two years; the second says it is up 4.3 per cent over one. An analyst states the base with the index, every time, because an index without its base is not a number.
EXAM TRAP   State the base
An index means nothing without its base year, and an exam answer that gives one without the other is incomplete.
If a question changes the base, every index in the series changes.
A base year with an unusual event in it distorts the whole series; say so rather than reading the trend.
