SC3-12  A company restates its 2023 results for an error. Its trend indexes on a 2023 base should:
A.	be left as they were, for consistency.
B.	be recalculated using the restated 2023 figures.
C.	be abandoned in favour of absolute changes.
D.	use the original figures with a note.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
3.5  What horizontal analysis cannot do
Horizontal analysis compares a company with itself, and that is both its strength and its limit.
It cannot tell you whether the level is right. Orontes's sales grew 15.4 per cent. Whether that is good depends on whether the market grew 5 per cent or 30 per cent, and the statements do not say.
It cannot separate price from volume. A 15.4 per cent rise in sales may be 15.4 per cent more jars, or the same jars at a 15.4 per cent higher price, or any combination. Chapter 4 shows what can and cannot be recovered, and Chapter 18 returns to it as the inflation problem.
It cannot survive a change of accounting. If the company moved from FIFO to LIFO during the series, the cost of goods sold line is measured two ways and the percentage change across the break is not a change in the business.
And it cannot cope with a negative or zero base. A company whose operating income moved from a loss of $500,000 to a profit of $500,000 has no meaningful percentage change: the arithmetic gives −200 per cent, which says nothing. Write "not meaningful" and give the absolute change.
Figure F503-08 lists the four limits with the question each one raises.
Figure F503-08. Four things a horizontal column will not tell you
@table
Limit | The question it raises | Where it is answered
The level may still be wrong | How did the market move? | Industry data, Chapter 1
Price and volume are mixed | Did we sell more, or charge more? | Chapter 4, and Chapter 18 on inflation
An accounting change breaks the series | Is this the same measurement? | Chapter 18
A zero or negative base | What does a percentage mean here? | Report the absolute change instead
@end
