Chapter summary
Figure F503-09 brings the chapter together.
Figure F503-09. Chapter 3 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Horizontal analysis compares each line with itself in an earlier year, and states the movement three ways: the absolute change, the percentage change with the earlier year as denominator, and a trend index on a base year set to 100. A large percentage on a small line means nothing, so the two are read together. Orontes grew 19.8 per cent in 2025, of which almost all the asset increase was cash, financed by retained earnings of $2,519,100, new shares of $1,500,000 and $400,000 more debt; liabilities grew more slowly than the company, so leverage fell. On the income statement, cost of goods sold grew faster than sales in both years, which is the falling gross margin seen from the other side, and operating income grew only 4.3 per cent in 2025 because of the impairment inside operating expenses. The base year must be normal, recent enough to be comparable, and restated when the company restates; an index without its base is not a number. Horizontal analysis cannot say whether the level is right, cannot separate price from volume, does not survive an accounting change, and has no meaning from a zero or negative base.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
Multiple-choice questions
P3-01  Horizontal analysis compares each line with:
A.	a total in the same statement.
B.	the same line in an earlier period.
C.	the industry average.
D.	a budget.
