A.	rise as a percentage of assets.
B.	fall as a percentage of assets.
C.	be unchanged as a percentage of assets.
D.	exceed 50 per cent of assets.
P3-08  An analyst calculates a percentage change on a line whose earlier amount was zero. The correct treatment is to:
A.	report an infinite percentage.
B.	report zero per cent.
C.	report the change as not meaningful and give the absolute amount.
D.	use the later amount as the denominator.
P3-09  Use Figure F503-05. Orontes's gross profit for 2024 is:
A.	11,840,000
B.	13,104,000
C.	14,700,000
D.	9,100,000
P3-10  Which of these breaks a trend series so that the percentage change across the break is not a change in the business?
A.	A rise in unit sales
B.	A change from FIFO to LIFO
C.	A dividend increase
D.	A share issue
Case-style practice: Barada's two years
Barada Wholesale's accountant hands the credit officer a horizontal analysis of its balance sheet. Inventory is up 41 per cent, accounts receivable are up 38 per cent, accounts payable are up 9 per cent, and the bank overdraft, which was nil a year ago, now stands at $240,000. Total assets are up 22 per cent. Sales for the year are up 11 per cent.
Required. Say what the pattern of these six movements suggests about Barada's year, naming the two that worry you most and why. Then state one figure you would ask for that is not in the list, and what it would settle. Continuous prose, under 250 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC3-1  Answer B. Percentage change divides the movement by the earlier amount.
A is wrong. Dividing by the later amount understates a rise and overstates a fall.
C is wrong. The average of the two is used in some turnover ratios, not here.
D is wrong. The base-year total is used in common-size analysis.
SC3-2  Answer B. An index of 128.0 against 100 is a rise of 28.0 per cent.
A is wrong. The index includes the original 100.
C is wrong. An index above 100 is a rise.
D is wrong. This divides the increase by ten.
SC3-3  Answer B. A 200 per cent rise on $8,000 is $16,000 in a company whose assets are $26,000,000.
A is wrong. Importance depends on the absolute amount as well as the percentage.
C is wrong. The earlier amount is $8,000, so the percentage is calculable.
D is wrong. The index is one valid statement, but it is no more informative here than the percentage.
SC3-4  Answer B. Cash rose $5,202,100 of a total asset increase of $5,227,600.
A is wrong. Receivables rose $384,000.
C is wrong. The investment rose $1,307,000, less than cash.
D is wrong. Inventory rose $150,000.
SC3-5  Answer B. Assets grew faster than liabilities, so liabilities are a smaller share of the company.
A is wrong. Borrowing grew more slowly than the company.
C is wrong. Total liabilities rose; they were not repaid.
D is wrong. Both percentages are consistent with the figures.
SC3-6  Answer B. A line growing more slowly than the base falls as a percentage of it.
A is wrong. A rise in the percentage needs growth faster than the base.
C is wrong. Equal growth would leave the percentage unchanged.
D is wrong. Both figures come from the balance sheet.
SC3-7  Answer B. If cost grows faster than sales, the margin between them narrows.
A is wrong. Gross profit still rose, by 12.2 per cent.
C is wrong. Operating income also rose, by 4.3 per cent.
D is wrong. Net sales is the base and is not a percentage of anything here.
SC3-8  Answer B. Sales accelerated, so a slowdown below the sales line must come from a cost.
A is wrong. Sales rose faster in 2025 than in 2024.
C is wrong. The index is consistent with the dollar amounts.
D is wrong. Tax affects net income, not operating income.
SC3-9  Answer C. Two points give a change; three or more give a direction.
A is wrong. One year gives nothing to compare.
B is wrong. Two years give a single movement, which may be an exception.
D is wrong. Five years is desirable but not the minimum.
SC3-10  Answer B. Every index in the series is divided by the base, so a distorted base distorts them all.
A is wrong. Nothing in the standards prescribes a base year for analysis.
C is wrong. There is no upper limit to an index.
D is wrong. The base year's figures are usually audited, but that is not the reason.
SC3-11  Answer B. The same amount gives different indexes on different bases, so the base is part of the statement.
A is wrong. Both are correctly calculated.
C is wrong. Operating income rose in both comparisons.
D is wrong. The earliest year is often chosen, but not because it must be.
SC3-12  Answer B. The series must be measured on one basis, so the restated figures are used.
A is wrong. Consistency with a figure now known to be wrong is not consistency.
C is wrong. Absolute changes have the same problem.
D is wrong. A note does not make a mixed-basis series comparable.
SC3-13  Answer C. A percentage change from a negative base is arithmetically defined and economically meaningless.
A is wrong. There is no basis for a positive 200 per cent.
B is wrong. The arithmetic gives this and it says nothing: the company improved.
D is wrong. The result is not a 100 per cent change on any defensible base.
SC3-14  Answer A. The sales line mixes price and volume, and the statements do not separate them.
B is wrong. Total assets are on the balance sheet and can be compared directly.
C is wrong. Dividends are visible in equity and in the cash flow statement.
D is wrong. The cost flow assumption is disclosed in the notes.
