Multiple-choice questions
P3-01  Answer B. Horizontal analysis compares a line with itself in an earlier period.
A is wrong. Comparison with a total in the same statement is common-size analysis.
C is wrong. The industry average is an external benchmark, not horizontal analysis.
D is wrong. Budget comparison is variance analysis.
P3-02  Answer B. 150,000 ÷ 2,300,000 = 6.5 per cent.
A is wrong. This divides by the later amount.
C is wrong. This is the index, not the change.
D is wrong. The base is positive, so the percentage is meaningful.
P3-03  Answer B. An index of 131.2 is a rise of 31.2 per cent.
A is wrong. The index includes the base of 100.
C is wrong. This divides the rise by ten.
D is wrong. This divides the rise by a hundred.
P3-04  Answer B. Cash rose $5,202,100, larger than any other movement in the figure.
A is wrong. Receivables rose $384,000.
C is wrong. Plant fell $1,198,000.
D is wrong. Retained earnings rose $2,519,100.
P3-05  Answer B. Cost of goods sold grew 17.2 per cent against sales of 15.4 per cent, so what is left over grew more slowly.
A is wrong. Operating expenses fall below gross profit and do not affect it.
C is wrong. Tax falls below operating income.
D is wrong. Interest falls below operating income.
P3-06  Answer B. A flood depresses the base, so later years are divided by a smaller number and the indexes overstate the improvement.
A is wrong. The base enters every index.
C is wrong. A depressed base flatters, it does not penalise.
D is wrong. The arithmetic still works; it is the meaning that fails.
P3-07  Answer B. Liabilities grew more slowly than the base, so they are a smaller share of it.
A is wrong. A rising share needs faster growth than the base.
C is wrong. Equal growth would hold the share constant.
D is wrong. Liabilities are 35.6 per cent of assets.
P3-08  Answer C. A percentage change from zero has no meaning; the absolute change does.
A is wrong. An infinite percentage is not a reportable figure.
B is wrong. Zero per cent asserts no change, which is false.
D is wrong. Changing the denominator changes the definition of the measure.
P3-09  Answer B. Net sales of 36,400,000 less cost of goods sold of 23,296,000.
A is wrong. This is 2023's gross profit.
C is wrong. This is 2025's gross profit.
D is wrong. This is 2024's total operating expenses.
P3-10  Answer B. A change of cost flow assumption measures the same goods a different way.
A is wrong. A rise in unit sales is a change in the business and belongs in the trend.
C is wrong. Dividends do not enter the income statement.
D is wrong. A share issue changes the balance sheet, not the measurement basis of the series.
Case-style set: Barada's two years
A good answer reads the six movements as one pattern.
Inventory up 41 per cent and receivables up 38 per cent against sales up only 11 per cent is the pattern that matters. Both working-capital assets grew about three and a half times as fast as the sales they exist to support. That is money going into stock that is not selling and into customers who are not paying, and it is not being funded by suppliers, because payables grew only 9 per cent. The overdraft of $240,000, where there was none before, is the consequence: the bank is now financing the gap.
The two most worrying are the inventory and the overdraft. The inventory movement is the cause and the overdraft is the symptom; a distributor that cannot convert stock to cash inside its season is borrowing to hold goods it has already paid for.
One figure to ask for: the ageing of the receivables, or failing that the days sales outstanding for each of the two years. Sales up 11 per cent with receivables up 38 per cent suggests collection has slowed, but it could also be a large sale made just before the year end. The ageing settles which, and the two have completely different implications for a credit line.
